<section class="section" id="getting-there" style="background:#f1e7d4">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="kicker">Plan your arrival</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Getting to {d.shortName}</h2>
    <p>Iqbal Park and the monument sit in the dense heart of the Walled City. The last stretch is pedestrian, so most visitors are dropped at a park gate and walk in. Below are the common ways in.</p>
    <div class="info-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="block-title">From the airport</h3>
        <p>{d.city}&rsquo;s international airport (Allama Iqbal International) lies roughly 25–30 km east of the old city. The most direct option is an app-based ride or metered taxi, which takes about 40–60 minutes depending on traffic. City buses and the metro network can also reach the old city with one or two transfers if you are travelling light.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="block-title">Public transit</h3>
        <p>{d.city} runs a bus rapid transit line (Metrobus) along the main boulevards, with stops a short walk or auto-rickshaw ride from the Walled City, and an elevated metro train (Orange Line) with stations within a few kilometres of the old city. From the nearest transit stop, a short taxi or rickshaw completes the trip to a park gate.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="block-title">Taxis &amp; ride-hailing</h3>
        <p>App-based ride-hailing and metered taxis can drop passengers close to the Iqbal Park gates. Request the drop-off for &ldquo;Iqbal Park, Minar-e-Pakistan&rdquo; and allow extra time in the evening when the area is busy. The final approach to the monument is on foot through the park.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3 class="block-title">Driving &amp; parking</h3>
        <p>If you drive, use the designated car and motorcycle parking areas inside Iqbal Park. The surrounding old-city streets are narrow and fill quickly on weekends and holidays, so arrive early. Ride-sharing drop-offs are easiest near the main gates.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section shell" id="facilities">
  <div class="kicker">On-site &amp; nearby</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Visitor Services &amp; Surrounding Facilities</h2>
  <p>This is an independent visitor-information resource. We describe the <em>types</em> of services available so you can plan; we do not endorse specific businesses. Availability and quality vary, so confirm on arrival.</p>
  <div class="info-grid">
    <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Restrooms (WC)</h3><p>Public restrooms are available within Iqbal Park near the main gates and the food-court area. Carry small change and tissues as a precaution.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Parking</h3><p>Designated parking areas for cars and motorcycles are provided inside the park. Expect fuller lots on weekends, public holidays and during evening illumination hours.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Food &amp; drink</h3><p>A range of food stalls, tea houses and casual restaurants operate around the Walled City and inside the park grounds. Try local snacks from busy, reputable stalls and drink bottled or sealed water.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Lodging</h3><p>{d.city} offers hotels and guesthouses across many budgets, concentrated near the Walled City, Mall Road and the central districts. Book ahead during peak travel seasons.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Shopping</h3><p>The bazaars and markets of the Walled City are known for textiles, handicrafts, spices and souvenirs. Bargaining is customary; keep valuables secure in crowded lanes.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Fuel &amp; EV charging</h3><p>Petrol stations and electric-vehicle charging points are available along the major approach roads. Top up before entering the old city, where fuel stops are limited.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section dark" id="seasons">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="kicker" style="color:#e5b55f">When to come</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Seasonal Visiting Strategy</h2>
    <p>Based on {d.city}&rsquo;s long-term climatological normals (multi-decade means), the monument and its park change a great deal through the year. Use the table to match your visit to weather, comfort and crowds.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap card">
      <table class="strategy">
        <thead><tr><th>Season</th><th>Weather &amp; comfort</th><th>Crowds</th><th>Air &amp; environment</th><th>Recommended focus</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Dec – Feb</b><br/><small>Winter</small></td><td>Mild and dry, days 7–20&deg;C; cold mornings/evenings.</td><td>Moderate; peaks on weekends and holidays.</td><td>Smog season — air quality often poorest of the year (Nov–Jan).</td><td>Daytime walks and photography on clear days; sensitive groups check air quality.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Mar – Apr</b><br/><small>Spring</small></td><td>Warm and pleasant, 20–35&deg;C; occasional dust/haze.</td><td>Rising as schools break.</td><td>Generally good; occasional haze.</td><td>All-round visiting; comfortable for families and long walks.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>May – Jun</b><br/><small>Pre-monsoon</small></td><td>Very hot, 35–45&deg;C; intense sun midday.</td><td>Lower in midday heat; evenings busier.</td><td>Hot, dry, dusty; poor air on calm days.</td><td>Mornings and after sunset only; hydrate and seek shade.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Jul – Sep</b><br/><small>Monsoon</small></td><td>Hot and humid; heavy showers and afternoon storms.</td><td>Variable; drops during heavy rain.</td><td>Lush, green park; paths can be slippery after rain.</td><td>Pack rain gear; watch forecasts for thunderstorms.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Oct – Nov</b><br/><small>Autumn</small></td><td>Most comfortable, 20–32&deg;C; clear skies return.</td><td>Higher as weather improves.</td><td>Air quality recovers; pleasant evenings.</td><td>Ideal window — combine monument with the old city.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="credits">Climate figures are indicative long-term norms for {d.city}; actual conditions vary year to year and within a season. The park&rsquo;s lawns and lake attract birds and peacocks — observe wildlife from a distance and do not feed it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section shell" id="itineraries">
  <div class="kicker">Make the most of your day</div>
  <h2 class="section-title">Itineraries</h2>
  <p>Choose a route that fits your group and pace. All options start from the monument inside Iqbal Park.</p>

  <h3 class="block-title">By type of traveller</h3>
  <div class="info-grid">
    <div class="card"><h4 class="route-name">Families with children</h4><p>Flat, paved park paths, shade, restrooms and food kiosks make this easy. Visit in the morning, let children enjoy the open lawns and the park lake, and keep the walk short. Strollers are manageable on main paths.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4 class="route-name">Photography &amp; nature</h4><p>Arrive at sunrise or stay for sunset when the tower is most photogenic. Capture the symmetrical minaret, its reflection in the park lake, and the Mughal skyline beyond. A tripod is useful in low light.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4 class="route-name">Low-effort / accessible</h4><p>Approach by vehicle to the nearest gate, use paved paths and benches, and keep to a compact loop close to the monument. Avoid the midday heat and crowded weekend evenings.</p></div>
  </div>

  <h3 class="block-title" style="margin-top:2.5rem">General routes</h3>
  <div class="info-grid">
    <div class="card"><h4 class="route-name">Half-day</h4><p>Enter Iqbal Park in the morning &rarr; visit the monument and gardens &rarr; walk a short stretch of the Walled City to Wazir Khan Mosque and Delhi Gate &rarr; return. About 3–4 hours at a relaxed pace.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h4 class="route-name">Full-day</h4><p>Morning at the monument and Iqbal Park &rarr; old-city walk (Delhi Gate, Wazir Khan Mosque, Shahi Hammam) &rarr; lunch in the Walled City &rarr; afternoon at Badshahi Mosque and Lahore Fort &rarr; return in the evening for the illuminated tower. 7–8 hours.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section dark" id="history">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="kicker" style="color:#e5b55f">Background &amp; stories</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">History &amp; Significance</h2>
    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p><strong>{d.fullName}</strong> stands on the ground where, on 23 March 1940, the All-India Muslim League passed the Lahore Resolution — the formal demand for a separate homeland for the Muslims of the subcontinent. The site was then part of Minto Park, later renamed Iqbal Park after the poet-philosopher Allama Iqbal.</p>
        <p>The foundation stone was laid on the same date in 1960, and the monument was completed in 1968. It was designed by architect Nasreddin Murat-Khan, who migrated to Pakistan and shaped a structure that blends Islamic, Mughal and modernist motifs.</p>
        <p>The tower rises about 70 metres through a series of petal-shaped platforms &ldquo;unfolding&rdquo; from the ground — widely read as a symbol of a nation coming into being. Inscriptions at the base include the text of the Pakistan Resolution and verses, and the different stone and marble tiers are said to reflect the stages of the struggle.</p>
        <p>Today the monument is a civic gathering point, a photography landmark and a reminder of the political moment that reshaped the region.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo-frame"><img src="https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/28/Minar-e-Pakistan%2C_Lahore.jpg/1280px-Minar-e-Pakistan%2C_Lahore.jpg" alt={`${d.fullName}, ${d.city}`} loading="lazy"/><figcaption class="credits">{d.fullName}, {d.city} — image via Wikimedia Commons</figcaption></figure>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="responsibility" style="background:#e7ddc8">
  <div class="shell">
    <div class="kicker">Visit responsibly</div>
    <h2 class="section-title">Respect &amp; Visitor Responsibility</h2>
    <div class="info-grid">
      <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Protect the monument</h3><p>Do not climb restricted areas or mark the stonework. Keep to paths and railings so the structure and gardens stay intact for everyone.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Dress &amp; conduct</h3><p>Modest attire is appropriate at this civic and cultural site. Keep noise low near gatherings and ask before photographing people.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Reduce your footprint</h3><p>Carry a reusable bottle, take litter home or use bins, and avoid single-use plastics where you can. The park is a shared green space.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h3 class="block-title">Health &amp; safety</h3><p>In winter smog season, sensitive groups should check air quality and carry a mask. Secure valuables in crowded areas, and hydrate in the summer heat.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

